why:
Small teams ship a mix of flying, walking and talking only by keeping the walkable area small and controlling where you arrive. Cloudpunk (about 8 people) lets you park only at set spots, Sable (6 core staff plus part-timers) baked its terrain ahead of time, A Short Hike was one person over about 3 months, and Messenger (2 people) is a three.js world of 17.5 MB, but stylised. Seamless interiors cost a lot even for big studios: in Assassin's Creed Unity, 25% of buildings had interiors and it took 9 studios; Kingdom Come: Deliverance 2 (about 250 people) skipped its church interiors; Red Dead Redemption 2's talk-to-anyone took about 2,000 people. Hence:
- a compact Kadıköy core;
- parametric interior shells with a capped number of places you can enter;
- dialogue written in ink, plus rule-matched ambient lines ('barks');
- no LLM-driven NPCs.

architecture:
Everything below is shared except the runtime column, which the phase-2 comparison decides. There is one world frame (±24 km); single-precision floats are enough.

WORLD LAYERS
- L0 Flight world (the existing three.js code): terrain, sea, sky, clouds, procedural city, landmarks, ships, cars. On foot it becomes a scaled-down far field beyond about 600 m.
- L1 City tiles (phases 08/09, re-scoped): the OSM city re-emitted as ~250 m chunks with building IDs, 3 levels of detail and entrances. It is the mid-distance view on foot and the view from the air.
- L2 Street layer (Kadıköy first, Galata–Karaköy second): ~100 m tiles, streamed within about 300 m of the player. Each tile holds:
  - façades with real depth: wall thickness, window reveals, çıkma balconies, railings, shutters, AC units, cornices;
  - shopfronts placed from OSM POIs, with fictional Turkish signs;
  - sidewalks, kerbs and cobbles;
  - instanced street furniture;
  - a light list (lamps and sign lights);
  - colliders that follow the true building footprint;
  - door and entrance records;
  - baked AO, plus light probes or lightmaps at 4 times of day.
  Low-detail versions of the tiles are what you see of Kadıköy from the air.
- L3 Interior cells: parametric shells (café, shop/meyhane, apartment stairwell, han/pasaj). The compiler fits each one to its building: width, depth, door offset, and openings cut into that façade tile. The first ones use a short fade at the door; they become seamless once one fitted example passes the test.

WORLD COMPILER (offline: Node plus Blender run from the command line)
- Inputs:
  - multi-area OSM: area.ts holds a list of areas, fetch-osm.mjs writes one file per area and also fetches entrance=*;
  - the existing three-free OSM code;
  - the façade-detail generators (props.ts, signs.ts, StreetSurface, furniture), run in Node.
- Blender: AO and lightmap bakes, reference renders of the same cameras (the content ceiling), hand-made hero pieces, character export.
- Shared output: plain glTF 2.0 with no meshopt or quantization, source textures, and JSON manifests (doors, POIs, lights, spawn points, seats, NPC slots).
- Packaging for each runtime:
  - web: glTF-Transform (KTX2 textures, meshopt) and recast navmesh tiles;
  - Godot: direct import, with a NavigationMesh baked from the same source geometry.
- Compiled tiles stay out of git history: GitHub release assets first, the Cloudflare R2 free tier when they grow. They are versioned by a manifest hash, and only the compiler and the manifests are committed.

ON-FOOT RENDERING (the test checks each item)
- Must have: TAA with reprojection, depth-based AO, SSR plus local reflection probes, at least 32 clustered local lights at night, baked probes or lightmaps (runtime re-bake only in small interiors), decals, occlusion culling, and HLOD.
- three.js: WebGPURenderer with TRAANode, GTAONode, SSRNode, ClusteredLighting and LightProbeGrid.
- Godot: TAA/FSR2, SSAO/SSIL, SSR, ReflectionProbe, LightmapGI or SDFGI, Decal, OccluderInstance3D and visibility ranges.
- In both, the fake rooms behind non-enterable windows come from porting the existing fRoom/fShop code in facade-glsl.ts.

GAMEPLAY SYSTEMS (three.js | Godot)
- Entity layer, possession service and input context stack (ui > dialogue > vehicle > on foot > flight): built by us | scene nodes plus InputMap.
- Walking character: Rapier 0.20 KinematicCharacterController | CharacterBody3D on Jolt.
- Cars (phase 7): Rapier DynamicRayCastVehicleController | VehicleBody3D or an add-on. The player's car joins the IDM traffic simulation as a leader, so AI cars react to it.
- Navigation and crowds: recast-navigation-js with DetourCrowd | NavigationServer3D with avoidance.
- Humans:
  - Quaternius Universal Base Characters with UAL 1/2 clips (CC0, one shared rig, no retargeting) for the crowd and stand-ins;
  - MPFB2 (CC0 base) for hero NPCs later;
  - mount, dismount and sit use cuts or fades plus UAL clips, with no custom keyframing.
- Dialogue:
  - ink files in story/*.ink, run by inkjs | godot-ink (which needs Godot's .NET build);
  - external functions trigger game actions, tags drive camera and animation;
  - Ink-Localiser line IDs for Turkish and English;
  - rule-matched barks for passers-by;
  - no LLM.
- Save: one versioned SaveDoc (context, district, interior, position, controlled entity, world flags, ink state JSON), stored in IndexedDB via idb-keyval with export/import | as JSON in user://. Schema migrations; autosave at every transition.

VERIFICATION
- walk-test.mjs, modelled on flight-test.mjs: fixed camera paths at eye level first, scripted interactions later.
- snap.mjs gains median and p99 frame times.
- Headless Chrome is launched with WebGPU enabled; otherwise it silently tests the WebGL2 fallback.
- Godot captures use --write-movie in a real window, queued like snap.mjs.

DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT: walking the whole city, entering every building, photoreal faces, voice acting, car damage, mobile support.

phases.goal:
In the winning runtime:
- entity layer and possession, input contexts;
- a character controller, third- and first-person cameras, UAL walk/jog/run;
- save v0.
You arrive on foot at the Kadıköy ferry pier. The dragon arrives in phase 6.

firstMilestone:
MILESTONE A: the fidelity proof (phases 0–2, about 4–6 sessions). One ~200 m strip from the Rıhtım into the çarşı, compiled once and viewed at eye level in three places: Blender (as a reference), three.js WebGPU and Godot 4.7 Forward+.

MILESTONE B: the first playable slice (phases 3–4, about 3 more sessions, in the winning runtime).
1. Step off the ferry.
2. Walk the strip.
3. Enter the café.
4. Order çay from the barista in a Turkish ink conversation.
5. A passer-by comments outside.
6. Save, reload, and everything is kept.
There is no driving in this slice.

THE TEST (the same in both runtimes, on the M2 Max)
- Scenes:
  - day, dusk, and night with at least 32 local lights;
  - 200 animated pedestrians in view and one NPC at 2–3 m;
  - a lit shop window seen from the street;
  - an open café door with the interior visible;
  - a walking capture along the strip at 1.4 m/s.
- Performance:
  - 'high' preset at 1600×900: median at least 60 fps, p99 frame time at most 25 ms, render scale at least 90%, no streaming hitch over 50 ms;
  - 'medium' preset at 1280×720, as a stand-in for weaker GPUs: median at least 60 fps;
  - web: first-visit street data at most 150 MB.
- Visuals: 10 shots paired with reference photos pass a fixed checklist, and you sign off on the runtime renders. The checklist:
  - façade depth is visible;
  - shopfront density and signage match the reference;
  - kerbs and sidewalks read correctly;
  - furniture and people are present;
  - corners show AO;
  - nothing visibly swaps detail level within 30 m;
  - no flicker on railings, shutters, cables or signs in the walking capture.
- Agent loop: how long an agent takes to make and verify one look change.

DECISION RULE (fixed now, to avoid sticking with a choice because of work already done)
- The Blender version fails → the kit is the problem. Fix the kit; do not change engines.
- Both runtimes pass → three.js WebGPU, because it keeps the web link and the flight game. The exception: you judge Godot clearly better side by side.
- Only Godot passes → the game moves to Godot on desktop. The three.js flight demo stays live as the web teaser until phase 6.
- Only three.js passes → three.js WebGPU.
- Neither passes while Blender does → re-run once with lower density (fewer lights, a smaller crowd, a shorter strip), then take whichever runtime came closer.

risks:
Licences:
- the free Quaternius tier is only 60–70% of each pack, so needed clips may be missing;
- realistic MPFB2 hair and clothes are mostly CC-BY and need attribution;
- ODbL share-alike applies to published tiles;
- shop names must be fictional.
I will list the exact clips and packs, with their licences, before each approval.

